ifs->read_bytes_pending -= len;
fserror_report_io(folio->mapping->host, FSERR_BUFFERED_READ,
		  folio_pos(folio) + off, len, error, GFP_ATOMIC);
Reason:
After decrementing `ifs->read_bytes_pending`, it may reach zero, allowing another completion to call `folio_end_read()`, which unlocks the folio. This enables truncate to set `folio->mapping` to NULL before the error reporting function dereferences `folio->mapping->host`, causing a NULL pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (error)
	fserror_report_io(folio->mapping->host, FSERR_BUFFERED_READ,
			  folio_pos(folio) + off, len, error, GFP_ATOMIC);
ifs->read_bytes_pending -= len;
Reason:
By moving the error reporting before the decrement of `read_bytes_pending`, the folio remains locked with a valid `mapping` pointer when the error is reported. This prevents the race with truncate that could set `folio->mapping` to NULL after the count reaches zero.

